"This course's very first seasonal lesson argued that a fifteen-day solar term makes advice specific enough to actually follow, unlike a three-month season. This lesson makes good on that claim with one concrete example: \"white dew,\" the solar term marking the point in early autumn when nighttime temperatures first drop enough for dew to visibly form on plants by morning — a specific, observable marker rather than a vague sense that \"autumn is coming.\" Because white dew sits early in the autumn stretch covered by an earlier lesson on the season's general advice, it sharpens that advice into something more actionable: the nights are cool enough to require covering the abdomen and feet while sleeping, since dampness and cold entering through the extremities is considered more likely once nighttime temperatures have specifically dropped this much, but the days are usually still warm enough that autumn's full dryness and its effect on the lung, discussed in the same earlier lesson, hasn't fully arrived yet. This is the payoff this course promised back in its first seasonal lesson: general advice like \"eat moistening foods in autumn\" is true but directionless, while a solar-term-specific version — introduce moistening foods gradually starting around white dew, before autumn's dryness peaks, rather than waiting until symptoms of dryness already appear — gives an actual window of time to act in. One solar term, examined closely, is what turns this entire unit's abstract seasonal framework into something a person could genuinely use on a specific day of the year.": "0fb951e20fdb",
